roups—in ways a one-size-fits-all public option might struggle to do. 4. **Complementarity.** Even if we had a public option, cooperatives could still offer a valuable alternative for people who prefer a member-governed, non-profit option. Prioritizing cooperatives doesn't mean opposing a public option—it means building a diverse ecosystem of affordable choices. So to your point: yes, the changes I'm describing are substantial. But I think cooperatives offer a path that is politically more resilient, more directly accountable to consumers, and more flexible than a public option alone. And if we're going to remake the system anyway, I'd rather build multiple tools for affordability and accessibility—not just one. Does that help clarify why I see cooperatives as worth prioritizing, even if they require big changes?
