23:00:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a completely fair and honest criticism. And honestly, I think you're right that many people *would* feel that way—and for good reason. If a cooperative is run poorly, if member meetings are ignored, if the board becomes self-perpetuating, then it really is just another bureaucracy with a friendlier name. I can't deny that. But let me push back just a little, because I think the difference isn't just in the name—it's in the legal structure and the incentives it creates. In a traditional insurance company, the legal duty of the board is to maximize profits for shareholders. If members are unhappy, that's a problem only if it threatens profits. In a cooperative, the board's legal duty is to serve the members. That doesn't guarantee good behavior—boards can be complacent or captured—but it means members have legal rights: the right to inspect records, the right to call special meetings, the right to vote out directors. Those aren't just friendly gestures; they're enforceable. Now, I'll be honest with you: most people won't exercise those rights. Most people just want their insurance to work and not think about it. And that's fine. But the *potential* for accountability matters, because it creates pressure on the board to actually listen—especially if a small, organized group of members starts paying attention. Compare that to a government option. If you're unhappy, your recourse is to vote for a different politician or write to an agency. That's a much longer, weaker chain of accountability. And if a giant for-profit insurer is treating you badly, your only real option is to switch plans—if you can afford to. So I'd say: cooperatives aren't a magic solution, and they can absolutely become bureaucratic and unresponsive. But they have a built-in mechanism for accountability that traditional insurers and government programs lack. Whether that mechanism is used depends on whether members care enough to engage. And that's a real limitation,